  FAQS: [
  {q:'How do I know if I need a septic tank or a soakaway?',a:"If your property isn't connected to mains drainage, you'll need a system to treat and disperse wastewater. A septic tank treats the waste, while a soakaway (drainage field) disperses treated water into the ground. We assess your site, soil and local regulations to recommend the right combination."},
  {q:'Do septic tank installations need building control approval?',a:'Yes — and the good news is we handle all of it for you. We complete the building department application forms, manage the site inspection visits and submit the compliance photos, so you never have to deal with the local authority.'},
  {q:'How much does it cost and how do I pay?',a:'We may not be the cheapest, but we guarantee the best service — and our quotes are fixed and all-inclusive (machinery, materials, labour and building reg fees, zero hidden costs). For installations we take 50% upfront to book the job, with the balance due two-thirds of the way through. A free site survey gives you the exact figure.'},
  {q:'How long does a typical installation take?',a:'Most residential septic tank or soakaway installations take between 2–5 days depending on ground conditions and access, from groundworks through to final connection and testing.'},
  {q:"What's involved in a CCTV drainage inspection?",a:'We run a waterproof camera through your drainage system to identify blockages, cracks, root ingress, or collapses — giving you a clear picture of the problem before any work begins, with a full report.'},
  {q:'Who will I actually speak to?',a:"You'll speak directly to Martin — the owner. No call centres, no being passed from person to person. If he's on site and misses your call, leave a voicemail and he personally guarantees to call you back within 24 hours (usually much faster)."}
],
